  </data>
  <data name="Help_PowerTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Power · 3s</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Power" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your power averaged over the last 3 seconds. In ERG, green, yellow, or red shows how close it is to the target.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_TargetTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Target</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Target" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The power the workout asks for. In ERG the trainer holds it at any gear or cadence.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_HeartRate" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Colored by heart rate zone. The zones come from Intervals.icu, or from the max heart rate in Settings.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Grade" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The virtual slope. With ERG off, it sets the resistance of each gear; ERG does not use it. On a route, the road sets it.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_NpTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>NP (normalized power)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Np" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The steady power that would have cost the same effort as the ride. Surges weigh more than in the plain average, so NP is higher than average power on an uneven ride.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_IfTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>IF (intensity factor)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_If" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>NP divided by FTP. About 0.65 is an easy ride, 0.85-0.95 is a hard tempo ride, and 1.0 is an all-out hour.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_TssTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>TSS (training load)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Tss" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Training Stress Score combines time and intensity. One hour at FTP is 100. Most riders recover from a day under about 150 by the next day.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_WPrimeTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>W′ (anaerobic reserve)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_WPrime" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>W′ ("W prime") is the work you can do above FTP, about 20 kJ for most riders. It drains while you ride above FTP and refills below it. The bar shows what is left now. The summary shows the lowest it got; near 0 means you rode to your limit.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_WorkTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Work</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Work" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Energy put into the pedals, in kJ. You burn about the same number of kcal.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Common_OnTarget" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>On target</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_OnTarget" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The share of the lap's seconds within 5% (at least 10 W) of the target.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Common_Best" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Best 5s / 1m / 5m / 20m</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Best" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Your highest average power over each duration in this ride.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_ZonesTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Power zones</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Zones" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Percent of FTP: Z1 up to 55, Z2 56-75, Z3 76-90, Z4 91-105, Z5 106-120, Z6 121-150, and Z7 above 150. Zones set in Intervals.icu replace these.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Chart" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>THE CHART</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_ChartText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A workout shows its steps as blocks: height is the target power, and color is the power zone. A route shows its hills by distance, colored blue downhill, gray on the flat, then green, yellow, orange, and red as the grade rises. The lines show what you rode; heart rate and grade use their own scales. The dashed line is your FTP, and the vertical line marks where you are now.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Controls" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>CONTROLS</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_HarderTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Harder / easier</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Harder" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Click + and − (Y and B on the Click V2), or press ↑ and ↓. With ERG off they shift the virtual gears; in ERG they move the target by 5 W.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_GradeKeys" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>← and → change it by 0.5%. On a route the road sets it.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Common_Erg" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>ERG</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Erg" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>E switches between ERG, where the trainer holds the target, and virtual gears, where you choose the effort.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_StepsTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Steps and laps</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Steps" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>S skips to the next step. L starts a new lap.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_PauseTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Pause and finish</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Pause" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>P or space pauses. The ride also pauses when you stop pedaling. Press Q or Finish twice to end and save the ride.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_RampTerm" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Ramp test</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Help_Ramp" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The target rises 20 W a minute until you cannot hold it. X ends the test early. The FTP estimate is 75% of your best minute.</value>
  </data>
